    "year": 2025,
    "examSource": "HSC Mathematics Advanced",
    "difficulty": "Medium",
    "image": "none",
    "questionText": "Prove that for all real numbers $x$, if $x > 1$ then $x^2 + x > 2x$.",
    "solution": "If $x > 1$ then $x^2 + x - 2x = x^2 - x = x(x - 1)$. Since $x > 1$, both $x > 0$ and $x - 1 > 0$, so their product $x(x - 1)$ is positive. Hence $x^2 - x > 0$, which means $x^2 + x > 2x$.",
    "tags": [
      "algebra",
      "inequalities",
      "proof",
      "long answer"
